{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Read the axis labels carefully (distance-time or speed-time?)",
   "Gradient of distance-time = speed",
   "Horizontal line on distance-time = stationary",
   "Gradient of speed-time = acceleration",
   "Area under speed-time = distance"
  ],
  "title": "How to Interpret Real-Life Graphs",
  "content": "<p><strong>Distance-time graphs</strong> show journeys. The gradient gives the <strong>speed</strong>: steeper = faster. A horizontal section means the object is stationary. Speed = distance ÷ time.</p><p><strong>Speed-time graphs</strong> (also called velocity-time graphs) show how speed changes. The gradient gives the <strong>acceleration</strong>. The <strong>area under the graph</strong> gives the total distance travelled.</p><p><strong>Conversion graphs</strong> let you convert between units (e.g. miles ↔ km, £ ↔ €). Read across from one axis to the line, then down to the other axis.</p><p>Common mistakes: confusing distance-time and speed-time graphs, reading the wrong axis, and forgetting that a flat line on a distance-time graph means <em>stopped</em> but a flat line on a speed-time graph means <em>constant speed</em>.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>A cyclist rides 12 km in 30 minutes, rests for 10 minutes, then rides 8 km in 20 minutes. What was their speed in the first section?</strong></p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Speed = distance ÷ time = 12 km ÷ 30 min = 0.4 km/min</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Convert: 0.4 × 60 = 24 km/h</p>"
 },
